Here is the MATLAB code to solve for "x":
syms a b c p q r x
eq1 = ap + bq + cr == 28;
eq2 = a + b + c == 10;
eq3 = x == a + 2*b - c/3;
sol = solve([eq1, eq2, eq3], [a, b, c]);
The "sol" variable will contain the solutions for "a", "b", and "c" that can be used to calculate the value of "x".

response:
Your approach is correct. Here's the MATLAB code to solve for "x":

```matlab
syms a b c p q r x
eq1 = ap + bq + cr == 28;
eq2 = a + b + c == 10;
eq3 = x == a + 2*b - c/3;
sol = solve([eq1, eq2, eq3], [a, b, c]);

% Extract the values of a, b, and c from the solution
a_val = double(subs(sol, 'a', ''));
b_val = double(subs(sol, 'b', ''));
c_val = double(subs(sol, 'c', ''));

% Calculate the value of x using the extracted values
x_val = a_val + 2*b_val - c_val/3;

disp(['The value of x is: ', num2str(x_val)]);
```

This code will output the value of "x" after solving the system of equations using the `solve()` function.

