Here's a JavaScript function that analyzes the given input data and returns the most relevant protocol:

```javascript
function determineProtocol(inputData) {
  const sourceIP = inputData.sourceIP;
  const destinationIP = inputData.destinationIP;
  const port = inputData.port;
  const payload = inputData.payload;

  if (isIPv6(sourceIP) && isIPv6(destinationIP)) {
    // If both source and destination IP addresses are IPv6, the most relevant protocol is IPv6
    return 'IPv6';
  } else if (port === 80) {
    // If the port number is 80, the most relevant protocol is HTTP
    return 'HTTP';
  } else if (payload === 'Hello World') {
    // If the payload is "Hello World", the most relevant protocol is UDP
    return 'UDP';
  } else {
    // If none of the above conditions are met, there is no most relevant protocol
    return null;
  }
}

function isIPv6(ipAddress) {
  // Regular expression pattern to check if an IP address is IPv6
  const ipv6Regex = /^([0-9a-fA-F]{1,4}:){7}([0-9a-fA-F]{1,4})$/;
  return ipv6Regex.test(ipAddress);
}
```

This function first checks if both source and destination IP addresses are IPv6 using the `isIPv6()` function. If they are, it returns 'IPv6'. If the port number is 80, it returns 'HTTP'. If the payload is "Hello World", it returns 'UDP'. If none of these conditions are met, it returns null.

