To retrieve the username from the JWT token in ASP.NET C# WebAPI, you can follow these steps:

1. Install the `Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens` package if you haven't already. You can do this by running the following command in the Package Manager Console:
```
Install-Package Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens
```
1. In your `Startup.cs` file, add the following code to configure the JWT token validation:
```csharp
public void ConfigureServices(IServiceCollection services)
{
    // ...

    services.AddAuthentication(options =>
    {
        options.DefaultAuthenticateScheme = CookieAuthenticationDefaults.AuthenticationScheme;
        options.DefaultSignInScheme = CookieAuthenticationDefaults.AuthenticationScheme;
        options.DefaultChallengeScheme = OpenIdConnectDefaults.AuthenticationScheme;
    })
    .AddCookie()
    .AddOpenIdConnect(options =>
    {
        options.Authority = "https://your-identity-server.com";
        options.ClientId = "your-client-id";
        options.ClientSecret = "your-client-secret";
        options.ResponseType = "code";
        options.SaveTokens = true;
        options.GetClaimsFromUserInfoEndpoint = true;
        options.UserInformationEndpoint = "https://your-identity-server.com/userinfo";
    });
}

public void Configure(IApplicationBuilder app, IWebHostEnvironment env)
{
    // ...

    app.UseAuthentication();
    app.UseAuthorization();
}
```
Replace `"your-identity-server.com"`, `"your-client-id"`, and `"your-client-secret"` with the appropriate values for your identity server and client.
1. In your controller or service, you can use the `User` object to retrieve the username from the JWT token. Here's an example:
```csharp
[HttpGet("username")]
public string GetUsername()
{
    var user = HttpContext.User;
    return user.Identity.Name;
}
```
This will return the username that is stored in the JWT token.

