Certainly! Here's a simple MATLAB code that simulates natural selection over several generations. In this case, we'll be selecting for individuals with a particular trait, "long legs," which gives them a higher chance of surviving in an environment with limited resources.

```MATLAB
% Define initial population
population_size = 100;
population = randi([0 1], population_size, 1); % Randomly generate traits (0 for short legs, 1 for long legs)

% Define fitness function
fitness = @(trait) 1 + trait; % Fitter individuals have higher fitness values

% Define number of generations
num_generations = 10;

% Simulate natural selection
for generation = 1:num_generations
    % Calculate fitness values for each individual
    fitness_values = fitness(population);

    % Select parents based on fitness
    selected_parents = zeros(population_size, 1);
    for i = 1:population_size
        selected_parents(i) = population(max(fitness_values));
    end

    % Generate offspring through crossover
    offspring = zeros(population_size, 1);
    for i = 1:population_size
        offspring(i) = (selected_parents(i) + population(i)) / 2;
    end

    % Mutate offspring with a small probability
    mutation_rate = 0.01;
    mutated_offspring = offspring .* (1 - mutation_rate) + population .* mutation_rate;

    % Update population for the next generation
    population = mutated_offspring;
end

% Display final population
disp(['Final population: ' num2str(population)]);
```

As you run this code, you'll notice that the frequency of long legs in the population increases over time, indicating natural selection at work.

