It seems like you want to distribute the planets evenly in space. You can achieve this by using a spatial partitioning algorithm such as a KD Tree or a Quadtree. These algorithms allow for efficient search and retrieval of objects within a specified range, which can be useful for distributing planets evenly in a 3D space.

Here's a simple implementation using a KD Tree:

```javascript
import { KDTree } from 'https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/kd-tree/0.8.2/kd-tree.min.js';

function distributePlanets(planets, minDistance) {
  const tree = new KDTree(planets, (a, b) => a.position.distanceToSquared(b.position));

  const distributedPlanets = [];

  while (planets.length > 0) {
    const closestPlanet = tree.search({ minDistance });
    distributedPlanets.push(closestPlanet);
    planets.splice(planets.indexOf(closestPlanet), 1);
  }

  return distributedPlanets;
}

const planets = generateRandomPlanets(5, 35);
const distributedPlanets = distributePlanets(planets, 35);
setPlanets(distributedPlanets);
```

This code will distribute the planets evenly in a 3D space with a minimum distance between them. You can adjust the `minDistance` parameter to control the spacing of the planets.

Note that this is a simplified implementation and may not be the most efficient way to distribute planets in a large-scale application. However, it should give you a good starting point.

Here's a simple React utility to remove widows from text. It uses the `React.useState` hook to keep track of the input text and the resulting text without widows. The component also provides a button to toggle the removal of widows.

```jsx
import React, { useState } from 'react';

const RemoveWidows = () => {
  const [inputText, setInputText] = useState('');
  const [outputText, setOutputText] = useState('');

  const handleRemoveWidows = () => {
    const words = inputText.split(' ');
    const widowedWords = [];

    for (let i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {
      const word = words[i];
      const lastLetter = word[word.length - 1];
      const nextWord = words[i + 1];

      if (nextWord && nextWord[0] === lastLetter) {
        widowedWords.push(word);
      }
    }

    const output = words.filter(word => !widowedWords.includes(word));
    setOutputText(output.join(' '));
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <textarea value={inputText} onChange={e => setInputText(e.target.value)} />
      <button onClick={handleRemoveWidows}>Remove Widows</button>
      <p>{outputText}</p>
    </div>
  );
};

export default RemoveWidows;
```

